Path('/app/answer.json').write_text('{"answer": "The mechanisms that ensure template processing functions registered during modular component registration are accessible during template rendering rely on Flask\'s deferred registration pattern and direct modification of the shared Jinja environment.\\n\\n**Deferred Registration Pattern:**\\nWhen a template filter is registered on a blueprint using `add_app_template_filter()` (located in `./src/flask/sansio/blueprints.py` lines 461-475), the blueprint doesn\'t immediately register the filter. Instead, it creates a deferred function that is stored in the blueprint\'s `deferred_functions` list:\\n\\n```python\\ndef register_template(state: BlueprintSetupState) -> None:\\n    state.app.jinja_env.filters[name or f.__name__] = f\\n\\nself.record_once(register_template)\\n```\\n\\n**Registration Phase:**\\nWhen the blueprint is registered with the application via `register_blueprint()`, the blueprint\'s `register()` method (lines 273-377 in `blueprints.py`) creates a `BlueprintSetupState` object that provides access to the application instance. All deferred functions are then executed with this state object:\\n\\n```python\\nstate = self.make_setup_state(app, options, first_bp_registration)\\n# ...\\nfor deferred in self.deferred_functions:\\n    deferred(state)\\n```\\n\\n**Direct Environment Modification:**\\nThe deferred function directly modifies the application\'s Jinja environment dictionary: `state.app.jinja_env.filters[name or f.__name__] = f`. This happens during blueprint registration, before any templates are rendered.\\n\\n**Rendering Phase:**\\nDuring template rendering, `render_template()` (in `./src/flask/templating.py` lines 138-150) accesses the application\'s Jinja environment through `current_app.jinja_env`:\\n\\n```python\\napp = current_app._get_current_object()\\ntemplate = app.jinja_env.get_or_select_template(template_name_or_list)\\nreturn _render(app, template, context)\\n```\\n\\nSince the filters were already registered in `app.jinja_env.filters` during blueprint registration, they are immediately available when Jinja2 resolves filter names during template rendering. The Jinja environment is shared across all templates, so filters registered by any blueprint are accessible to all templates rendered by the application.\\n\\n**Key Architectural Points:**\\n1. **Decoupling**: Filter definition (blueprint creation time) is separated from filter registration (blueprint registration time) and filter usage (template rendering time)\\n2. **Shared Environment**: All blueprints register filters into the same `app.jinja_env.filters` dictionary, ensuring global availability\\n3. **Deferred Execution**: The `record_once()` method ensures filters are only registered once, even if a blueprint is registered multiple times\\n4. **No Runtime Resolution**: Filters don\'t need special resolution logic during rendering because they\'re already in the environment\'s filter dictionary\\n\\nThis architecture ensures that template processing functions defined at the blueprint level are properly integrated into the application\'s template system without requiring blueprint-specific context during template rendering."}' + '\n')
